itle:"Questions with か",pattern:"… ですか。",explanation:"Adding か to the end of a polite sentence turns it into a question — no change in word order. Question words such as 何 (what), どこ (where), いくら (how much) and 何番線 (which platform) sit where the answer would go.",examples:[["いくらですか。","How much is it?"],["{駅|えき}はどこですか。","Where is the station?"],["おすすめは{何|なん}ですか。","What do you recommend?"]],stage:1},{id:"g_request",title:"Requests: 〜をください / 〜をお願いします",pattern:"[thing] を ください / お願いします。",explanation:"を marks the thing you are asking for. ください means “please give me”. お願いします is a slightly softer, very common way to request something, and also works on its own to accept an offer (“yes, please”). Both are polite enough for shops and restaurants. In casual speech を is often dropped: お水お願いします.",examples:[["これをください。","This one, please."],["お{水|みず}をお{願|ねが}いします。","Water, please."],["お{会計|かいけい}をお{願|ねが}いします。","The bill, please."]],stage:1},{id:"g_tsu",title:"Counting things: 一つ、二つ、三つ",pattern:"[thing] を [number]つ …",explanation:"Japanese uses counters. The general-purpose “つ” series (ひとつ、ふたつ、みっつ、よっつ…) works for most items when ordering. The number usually comes after the thing and its particle: ラーメンを一つ.",examples:[["ラーメンを{一|ひと}つお{願|ねが}いします。","One ramen, please."],["{餃子|ぎょうざ}を{二|ふた}つください。","Two gyoza, please."]],stage:1},{id:"g_ikaga",title:"Polite offers: 〜はいかがですか",pattern:"[thing] は いかがですか。",explanation:"いかが is a polite “how (about)”. Staff use 〜はいかがですか to offer you something. Answer お願いします to accept, or 大丈夫です / 結構です to decline politely.",examples:[["{飲|の}み{物|もの}はいかがですか。","Would you like something to drink?"]],stage:1},{id:"g_yuki",title:"〜行き — “bound for”",pattern:"[place] 行き の 電車",explanation:"行き after a place name means “bound for”. の then links it to the noun it describes: 月見都行きの電車 = “the Tsukimiyako-bound train”. In announcements 行き is read ゆき.",examples:[["{月見都|つきみやこ}{行|ゆ}きの{電車|でんしゃ}","the train bound for Tsukimiyako"],["まもなく、{3番線|さんばんせん}に{月見都|つきみやこ}{行|ゆ}きの{電車|でんしゃ}がまいります。",